# Loghound — application-root safety net.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
#
# The supported deployment serves ONLY the public/ directory, with the vhost
# using AllowOverride None. In that configuration this file is never read and
# nothing here matters.
#
# It exists for the configuration we do NOT control. Hosting control panels and
# server-management platforms routinely (re)generate vhosts from a generic
# template, and a generic template has no way to know that this application
# keeps its document root one level down. The common result is a vhost whose
# DocumentRoot is the application root, with AllowOverride All. That would
# expose the runtime state database, the git metadata, and anything else in the
# tree to anyone who guesses a filename.
#
# Because those generated templates set AllowOverride All, this file activates
# in precisely the situation that is dangerous, and stays inert otherwise. It is
# defence in depth, not the primary control.
#
# The primary control remains: point the document root at public/.
#
# DELIBERATELY NOT DONE HERE: remapping .php to a plain-text type. That is the
# usual "disable PHP" recipe and it is wrong for this tree. config/loghound.php
# holds the API key and the beacon HMAC secret; served as text those leak, while
# executed it prints nothing because of its own PHP_SAPI guard. Turning
# execution into source disclosure would make this file harmful rather than
# protective, so handler and type mappings are left completely alone.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Deny everything. This is the whole point of the file.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
<IfModule mod_authz_core.c>
    # Apache 2.4+
    Require all denied
</IfModule>

<IfModule !mod_authz_core.c>
    # Apache 2.2 and earlier
    Order allow,deny
    Deny from all
</IfModule>

# No directory listings, whatever the generated vhost happens to say.
Options -Indexes
